{
  "dom_id": "step-q184493",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,2,3}\\times (0,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?",
  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={-1,2,3}\\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-1,2,$ or $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x{-1,2,3},\\,y\\in (0,1)}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/1e09e88b9f8bd472c1fd74b4d77a7aefff9b1170787899bc78fcf56e79518436.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/3f4ecac159eba9f49d6745ac2a46956fd19b68f0520974a6c618ebc1f118588a.png)\n\nNow, consider ${S}_{3}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(3,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/969ee218899817fe5bf497369fb6b2e5fa360fba0fbd4733c442c42208fa566c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the three line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/7f0ad1b126502e27710bcc97a72c4eec889a74f7f6dfac6449d98710ea16e16b.png)",
